---
import Docs from '../../../layouts/Docs.astro'
import RecipeCard from '../../../components/RecipeCard.astro'

const installed = `style:
  label:
    font: 'Inter, Arial, sans-serif'
    weight: 700
    size: 44`

const fontFile = `style:
  label:
    fontUrl: fonts/Inter-Bold.woff2
    font: 'Inter'
    weight: 700`

const stylesheet = `style:
  label:
    fontUrl: fonts/inter.css
    font: 'Inter'`

const remote = `style:
  label:
    fontUrl: https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter:wght@700&display=swap
    font: 'Inter, Arial, sans-serif'`

const stroke = `style:
  label:
    fill: '#FFFFFF'
    stroke: '#DC2626'
    strokeWidth: 6`
---

<Docs
  title="How to use a custom label font"
  description="Draw callout labels in your product's typeface, whether it is installed on the machine, shipped in the repository, or hosted."
>
  <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold">Use a custom label font</h1>

  <p class="text-lg">
    By default, labels are drawn in a generic sans-serif. This guide draws them in your product's
    typeface instead. Choose the section that matches where the font lives.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">The font is installed on the machine</h2>

  <p>Name it under <code>style.label.font</code>:</p>

  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={installed} file="shotlist.config.yaml" />
  </div>

  <p>
    The name has to be resolvable by the browser doing the drawing. A family that is not installed
    falls back silently, so shotlist measures the rendered text and warns when the result is not the
    typeface you asked for. It is a warning rather than an error, because a fallback still produces
    a usable image.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">The font file is in the repository</h2>

  <p>
    Point <code>fontUrl</code> at the file. A <code>.woff2</code>, <code>.woff</code>,
    <code>.otf</code> or <code>.ttf</code> works:
  </p>

  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={fontFile} file="shotlist.config.yaml" />
  </div>

  <p>
    shotlist declares the face for you, under the first real family named in
    <code>style.label.font</code> and at that label's weight. A stack made only of generic families such
    as <code>sans-serif</code> is rejected, because there is no name to declare the file under.
  </p>

  <p>
    One file is one face. If you need a regular and a bold, write a stylesheet and point at that
    instead:
  </p>

  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={stylesheet} file="shotlist.config.yaml" />
  </div>

  <p>
    The stylesheet is read from disk and inlined, along with the font files it references. Paths
    resolve from the configuration file's folder, as every path in the configuration does.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">The font is hosted</h2>

  <p>Give <code>fontUrl</code> an <code>http(s)</code> or <code>data:</code> URL:</p>

  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={remote} file="shotlist.config.yaml" />
  </div>

  <p>
    The stylesheet is fetched when the callouts are drawn, so the machine taking screenshots needs
    network access at that moment. The wait is bounded by <code>site.timeout</code>; past that,
    labels are drawn in whatever the browser had and the run warns. A slow font server delays a run,
    it never holds one open.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">Adjust the outline around the glyphs</h2>

  <p>
    Labels are drawn filled, with an outline that separates them from the screenshot underneath:
  </p>

  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={stroke} file="shotlist.config.yaml" />
  </div>

  <p>
    <code>strokeWidth</code> is centered on the glyph outline, so half of it is painted under the fill.
    A value of 6 reads as a 3-pixel outline. Tools that draw the stroke entirely outside the glyph need
    roughly double the number here to match.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>stroke</code> defaults to <code>style.color</code>, so setting the annotation color alone
    keeps labels consistent with boxes and arrows.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">Related</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href="/docs/reference/configuration#style">Configuration reference: <code>style</code></a>
    </li>
    <li><a href="/docs/reference/callouts">Callouts and masks reference</a></li>
  </ul>
</Docs>
